 \section{Introduction}
 \label{sec:introduction}
 
-Stochastic epidemic simulation in structured populations must account for dynamics occurring at different spatial and structural scales. Within communities, transmission can depend on detailed contact networks and local stochastic interactions \citep{keeling2005,pastorsatorras2015,kiss2017}, whereas transmission between communities is often represented through aggregate mobility flows or weighted coupling networks \citep{balcan2009,barbosa2018human}. This mismatch in resolution creates a multiscale simulation problem in which fine-grained local dynamics must be coupled through a coarser inter-community representation.
+Epidemics spread at more than one scale. Inside a community, infection may pass through a detailed network of contacts between people \citep{keeling2005,pastorsatorras2015,kiss2017}. Between communities, the available data are often much less detailed. For example, they may give only passenger numbers, commuting flows, or a weighted link between two places \citep{balcan2009,barbosa2018human}. We therefore need a model that can use a detailed network inside each community and simpler data between communities.
 
-In practice, such a mismatch is common because within-community contact networks may be observed, reconstructed or synthesised from partial information \citep{eubank2004,danon2011}, whereas interactions between communities may be available only as commuting volumes, mobility counts or other aggregate coupling measures \citep{barbosa2018human,chang2021}. Importantly, an aggregate measure of connectivity between two communities does not uniquely determine the microscopic cross-community contacts that produced it. Consequently, the aggregate representation omits information about which individuals constitute the interface between communities and how their states affect inter-community transmission.
+This situation is common in practice. A local contact network may be observed, reconstructed, or generated to match known properties such as clustering and mean shortest-path length \citep{eubank2004,danon2011}. In contrast, links between communities may be known only through transport, migration, commuting, or mobile-phone data \citep{barbosa2018human,chang2021}. Such data can tell us the total strength of connection between two communities, but not which people form the connection. We may therefore know the total coupling without knowing the bridge nodes or their infection states.
 
-When the complete contact network is available, epidemic dynamics can be formulated as a continuous-time Markov chain (CTMC) and sampled exactly by Gillespie-type event-driven methods \citep{gillespie1977exact,gibson2000}. Extensions for temporal, large and sparse networks can substantially improve computational efficiency \citep{vestergaard2015,kuryliak2025simulating}, but they still require the relevant microscopic interaction channels to be explicitly represented. At the opposite end of the modelling spectrum, metapopulation approaches naturally describe interactions between communities through aggregate flows, but usually aggregate the within-community state as well \citep{colizza2007reaction,colizza2008,balcan2010modeling,ajelli2010}. This leaves an intermediate simulation problem: how to retain explicit stochastic dynamics within communities while coupling them through an inter-community interface that is available only in aggregate form.
+If every contact is known, a continuous-time Markov chain (CTMC) can describe the epidemic, and a Gillespie-type method can simulate it exactly \citep{gillespie1977exact,gibson2000}. Efficient methods are also available for temporal, large, and sparse networks \citep{vestergaard2015,kuryliak2025simulating}. However, these methods still need the individual contacts that may transmit infection. Metapopulation models use aggregate flows between communities, but usually also replace the detailed local network by population counts \citep{colizza2007reaction,colizza2008,balcan2010modeling,ajelli2010}. Our problem lies between these two cases: we want to keep node-level stochastic spread inside every community while using only aggregate information between communities.
 
-A two-scale simulator for this intermediate regime was outlined in a short workshop paper \citep{kuryliak2025towards}. Each community is evolved by an independent stochastic micro-simulator, while a macro layer generates cross-community imports from state-dependent aggregate hazards and places each import in the receiving community. This architecture preserves detailed stochastic dynamics within communities while aggregating only the coupling interface. The workshop paper motivated the architecture and sketched its layers, but did not quantify the fidelity loss caused by replacing microscopic cross-community channels by aggregate coupling or by the rule used to place imported events. We therefore present the method here in a self-contained, implementation-level form.
+The basic two-scale simulator was presented in an earlier workshop paper \citep{kuryliak2025towards}. It runs one stochastic local simulator for each community. A separate macro layer uses the current community states and the aggregate coupling to generate infections that enter from another community. The present article gives a full description of this method and studies its approximation error. In particular, we ask what is lost when real cross-community edges and their endpoints are replaced by an aggregate connection, and what is lost when an imported infection is placed on a selected susceptible node.
 
-The central question of this article is whether an aggregate inter-community interface can reproduce the stochastic behaviour of the corresponding full-network CTMC with sufficient fidelity, for which observables and in which network regimes. The answer depends on information discarded by the interface: which nodes carry the inter-community channels and whether they are currently infected or susceptible, where imported infections are placed, and, for thin interfaces, how many distinct microscopic crossings are physically available. We address these effects analytically and through controlled experiments on synthetic networks in which the complete microscopic network is known to the experimenter but only its aggregate representation is passed to the two-scale simulator. The purpose is methodological rather than empirical calibration of a particular epidemic system.
+Our main question is: when does this aggregate interface reproduce the behaviour of the full-network CTMC well enough for the quantity of interest? We study arrival times, their distributions, and the physical validity of the transmission route. In particular, similar arrival times do not prove that the route was physically possible: the aggregate model can export infection before any real bridge node has been infected. The complete microscopic network is known in our controlled experiments, so it can be used as a reference. The two-scale simulator receives only the local networks and aggregate connections. These experiments test the method; they are not a calibration of one particular epidemic.
 
-The contributions are as follows.
+This article makes four contributions.
 \begin{enumerate}
-\item A complete, implementation-level specification of the two-scale method, including the grid-synchronised hazard-integral scheduler used in practice (Section~\ref{sec:method}, Algorithm~\ref{alg:scheduler}).
-\item An exact characterisation of the ideal method as full-network simulation on a surrogate network with uniform all-to-all interface coupling (Proposition~\ref{prop:surrogate}). It yields a taxonomy of discrepancies and a stochastic-ordering result for single exports (Proposition~\ref{prop:ordering}).
-\item A controlled comparison with full-network Gillespie simulation on single-export benchmarks and on chains of communities, using permutation baselines that separate approximation error from sampling variability (Sections~\ref{sec:design} and~\ref{sec:results}).
-\item A practical criterion: the ratio of the within-community spread of infection times to the single-edge crossing time organises the observed discrepancies across community sizes, internal structures and interface widths (Section~\ref{sec:res_descriptor}).
+\item We give a complete description of the two-scale simulator, including the time-grid scheduler used in the experiments (Section~\ref{sec:method}, Algorithm~\ref{alg:scheduler}).
+\item We show exactly what the ideal continuous-time method simulates. It is exact for a \emph{surrogate network}: the real cross-community edges are replaced by weak all-to-all connections with the same total weight (Proposition~\ref{prop:surrogate}). It is not, in general, exact for the original physical network. We then identify the resulting sources of error and analyse a single export (Proposition~\ref{prop:ordering}).
+\item We compare the method with full-network Gillespie simulation for single exports and chains of communities. Permutation baselines show how much difference can arise from finite sampling alone (Sections~\ref{sec:design} and~\ref{sec:results}).
+\item We give an empirical indicator of accuracy for our benchmarks. It compares the spread of infection times inside a community with the typical time needed to cross one interface edge (Section~\ref{sec:res_descriptor}).
 \end{enumerate}
 
-The study is restricted to susceptible--infected (SI) dynamics on static synthetic networks and a fixed synchronisation step. Section~\ref{sec:related} relates the method to existing simulation approaches; Section~\ref{sec:method} specifies it; Section~\ref{sec:analysis} analyses what the aggregate interface preserves; Sections~\ref{sec:design} and~\ref{sec:results} describe the experiments and their results; Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses practical use, cost and limitations.
+We consider susceptible--infected (SI) dynamics on static synthetic networks and use one fixed synchronisation step. Section~\ref{sec:related} explains the relation to earlier methods. Section~\ref{sec:method} describes the simulator. Section~\ref{sec:analysis} explains what the aggregate interface preserves and loses. Sections~\ref{sec:design} and~\ref{sec:results} present the experiments and results. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses use, cost, and limitations.
+
