 \section{Related work}
 \label{sec:related}
 
-\paragraph{Exact and approximate stochastic simulation.}
-Gillespie's direct method samples a CTMC exactly from the current propensities \citep{gillespie1977exact}; the next reaction method organises the same computation around per-channel clocks \citep{gibson2000}. When propensities vary in time, exact simulation can be expressed through the random-time-change representation of a counting process \citep{ethier1986}: an event fires when its integrated intensity reaches an independent exponential threshold. \citet{anderson2007} used this representation to derive the modified next reaction method for time-dependent propensities and delays. The macro clock of the two-scale simulator uses the same cumulative-hazard principle, but with an intensity driven by the evolving stochastic states of the local engines. Network-oriented implementations update only the propensities affected by an event \citep{vestergaard2015,kuryliak2025simulating}; the local engines used here follow \citet{kuryliak2025simulating}.
+\paragraph{Simulation of stochastic events.}
+Gillespie's direct method draws the next event and its time from the current event rates \citep{gillespie1977exact}. The next reaction method keeps a separate clock for each possible event \citep{gibson2000}. Both methods are exact for the CTMC they simulate. When an event rate changes with time, its rate can be accumulated until it reaches a random threshold \citep{ethier1986}. \citet{anderson2007} used this idea for systems with time-dependent rates and delays. Our macro clock uses the same idea, but its rate changes because the local epidemic states change. Network simulators can save work by updating only the rates affected by the latest event \citep{vestergaard2015,kuryliak2025simulating}. Our local simulators use the implementation of \citet{kuryliak2025simulating}.
 
-Hybrid and multiscale stochastic simulation algorithms accelerate systems with widely separated reaction rates by approximating fast channels \citep{haseltine2002,cao2005slow}. The two-scale method differs in what it approximates: it does not average fast reaction channels, but removes microscopic information about which inter-community channels are active. As shown later, its accuracy is nevertheless organised by a separation of time scales between internal spread and interface crossing.
+Some multiscale methods speed up chemical-reaction models by approximating reactions that occur much faster than others \citep{haseltine2002,cao2005slow}. Our method removes a different kind of detail. It keeps the local infection events, but does not know which individual cross-community edges are currently able to transmit. Even so, the relative speeds of local spread and cross-community transmission help explain its error.
 
-\paragraph{Metapopulation and mobility models.}
-Metapopulation models couple subpopulations through mobility flows and have been used to study invasion thresholds and global spread \citep{colizza2007reaction,colizza2008,balcan2009,balcan2010modeling}; high-resolution mobility networks can likewise be combined with local transmission models \citep{chang2021}. Agent-based models resolve individuals throughout \citep{eubank2004,kerr2021}, and \citet{ajelli2010} compared the two paradigms. In contrast, the present method preserves explicit node-level stochastic dynamics within communities and applies coarse-graining only at the inter-community interface, where microscopic transmission channels are replaced by aggregate stochastic coupling.
+\paragraph{Models with several communities.}
+Metapopulation models represent each subpopulation by counts and connect the subpopulations by mobility flows. They have been used to study global spread and the conditions for invasion \citep{colizza2007reaction,colizza2008,balcan2009,balcan2010modeling}. Detailed mobility data can also be combined with local transmission models \citep{chang2021}. Agent-based models instead represent individuals throughout the model \citep{eubank2004,kerr2021}. \citet{ajelli2010} compared these two broad approaches. The method studied here keeps individual nodes and contacts inside each community, but replaces the unknown individual contacts between communities by one aggregate stochastic interface.
 
 \paragraph{Two levels of mixing and co-simulation.}
-Epidemic models with two levels of mixing combine local contacts within small groups with uniform global mixing \citep{ball1997}. The surrogate network of Proposition~\ref{prop:surrogate} has a related structure, with uniform mixing restricted to pairs of communities and weighted by their aggregate coupling. From a simulation-engineering perspective, the two-scale scheduler is also related to co-simulation: independently solved subsystems are advanced on a communication grid and exchange a limited set of coupling variables \citep{gomes2018cosim}. The distinction is that the interface here generates stochastic event times and target locations rather than merely exchanging state variables, and therefore directly determines part of the simulated event process.
+Some epidemic models combine contacts in small local groups with uniform mixing at a wider level \citep{ball1997}. Our surrogate network is related to this idea, but uniform mixing is used only between connected pairs of communities, and its strength is set by their aggregate weight.
+
+The implementation also has features of co-simulation. In co-simulation, separate subsystem solvers move forward on a shared time grid and exchange a limited amount of information \citep{gomes2018cosim}. Here, the local community simulators are those subsystems. They send their infected and susceptible counts to the macro layer. In this implementation, those exchanged counts are used to draw a cross-community infection time and then to choose its receiving community and target node. The scheduler can therefore treat the local simulators as black boxes.
+
