-\subsection{Local engines}
+\subsection{Local simulators}
 \label{sec:local}
 
-Each community is simulated by a separate engine that samples the CTMC of its own graph $G_i$: infection along channels in $E_{ii}$ and removal of infected nodes, with the propensities of~\eqref{eq:full_generator} restricted to $V_i$. The engines use the incremental Gillespie implementation of \citet{kuryliak2025simulating}, in which each node stores the weight of its contacts with infected neighbours and only the propensities affected by an event are updated. Each engine owns a private pseudorandom stream.
+Each community has a separate Gillespie simulator for its local graph $G_i$. It applies the infection and removal rates in~\eqref{eq:full_generator} using only the local edges $E_{ii}$. The implementation updates only rates affected by the latest event, following \citet{kuryliak2025simulating}. Each local simulator has its own random-number stream.
 
-An engine supports two operations used by the macro layer: advancing its clock to a prescribed time, applying only events that occur before it, and an \emph{import}, which sets a given susceptible node to $I$. An import changes the local state and propensities, so the engine discards its pending next event and samples a new one from the updated state. This restart is distributionally valid for the CTMC because the relevant waiting times are exponential. Equivalently, a random-time-change implementation may retain the unconsumed exponential hazard budget across the import. Conditional on the complete sequence of import times and target nodes, the local engine therefore has exactly the law of the within-community CTMC with those imports applied as exogenous $S\to I$ jumps. The approximation enters through how the import process is generated, not through the local CTMC simulation itself.
+The macro layer can ask a local simulator to move to a specified time. It can also import an infection by changing one selected susceptible node to $I$. An import changes the local rates. The corrected implementation keeps the unused part of the exponential threshold for the next local event and recalculates its waiting time using the new total rate. It then selects the event type and node using the updated rates. A new local threshold is drawn only if there is no existing one. This follows the same accumulated-rate principle explained in Section~\ref{sec:timing}. For prescribed import times and target nodes, the local simulator follows the CTMC rules of its own network. The approximation comes from the way the macro layer generates and places imports.
 
 \subsection{Macro layer}
 \label{sec:macro}
 
-The macro layer treats communities as nodes of a weighted graph with weights $W_{ij}$. Given the current summaries, it assigns to every ordered pair $i\neq j$ the hazard
+The macro layer represents each community by its current fractions and represents each directed connection by $W_{ij}$. Its rate for an infection from community $i$ to community $j$ is
 \begin{equation}
 \label{eq:macro_rate}
-\lambda_{ij}(t)=\beta_{\mathrm{out}}\,W_{ij}\,\ibar_i(t)\,\sbar_j(t),
+\lambda_{ij}(t)=\beta_{\mathrm{out}}W_{ij}\ibar_i(t)\sbar_j(t),
 \qquad
 \Theta(t)=\sum_{i\neq j}\lambda_{ij}(t).
 \end{equation}
-A macro event represents one imported infection from some community into another. Conditional on a macro event at time $t$ with $\Theta(t)>0$, the pair $(i,j)$ is selected with probability $\lambda_{ij}(t)/\Theta(t)$, and a target $v\in S_j(t)$ is drawn from a placement rule $\Pi_{ij}(\cdot\mid X(t))$. The default rule used throughout this article is uniform placement, $\Pi_{ij}(v\mid X(t))=1/s_j(t)$. Degree-weighted or boundary-aware rules are possible when such information is available. The implementation also contains a multiplicative calibration factor for~\eqref{eq:macro_rate}, which is set to one in all experiments.
+The factors have a direct meaning: $\beta_{\mathrm{out}}W_{ij}$ gives the total connection strength, $\ibar_i$ is the infected fraction in the source, and $\sbar_j$ is the susceptible fraction in the receiving community. The sum $\Theta$ is the rate of any cross-community infection.
 
-Equation~\eqref{eq:macro_rate} is an effective aggregate intensity rather than, in general, the exact rate of the realised microscopic bundle. Conditional on the community counts, it equals the expected bundle rate when the inter-community endpoints are assigned uniformly at random, before conditioning on their realised identities. It coincides with the actual rate when the interface is uniform all-to-all coupling (Corollary~\ref{cor:exact}) and is otherwise an approximation (Section~\ref{sec:analysis}). The macro layer is not autonomous: $\Theta$ changes whenever a local event or import changes a community count. Because the summaries are piecewise constant between such events, so is $\Theta$.
+When such an event occurs, the pair $(i,j)$ is selected with probability $\lambda_{ij}/\Theta$. Write $\Pi_{ij}(v\mid x)$ for the probability of selecting target node $v$ when the current state is $x$. The default rule chooses uniformly from the susceptible set $S_j$, so $\Pi_{ij}(v\mid x)=1/s_j$ for every susceptible node $v\in S_j$. Other target rules can be used if bridge-node or boundary information is available. The optional calibration factor in the implementation is one in all experiments.
 
-\subsection{Hazard-integral timing and synchronisation}
+This aggregate rate is generally an approximation to the rate across the real physical edges. For fixed community counts, it is the expected rate if the endpoints of those edges are placed uniformly at random, before their identities, states, and epidemic history are known. It becomes the actual rate for the uniform all-to-all surrogate interface described in Section~\ref{sec:analysis}. The rate $\Theta$ changes after any local event or imported infection that changes a community count.
+
+\subsection{Timing and synchronisation}
 \label{sec:timing}
 
-Let $t_0$ be the time of the previous macro event and $E\sim\Exp(1)$ an independent threshold. In the ideal continuous-time construction, the next macro event occurs at
+After a macro event at time $t_0$, draw a random threshold $E\sim\Exp(1)$: an exponential random number with mean one. Starting from zero, accumulate the total macro rate $\Theta$. The next macro event occurs at the first time $t^\ast$ when
 \begin{equation}
 \label{eq:hazard_integral}
-t^\ast=\inf\Bigl\{t>t_0:\int_{t_0}^{t}\Theta(s)\,ds\ge E\Bigr\}.
+\int_{t_0}^{t^\ast}\Theta(s)\,ds=E.
 \end{equation}
-Conditional on the realised micro-layer trajectories, $\Theta(t)$ is a predictable intensity path and~\eqref{eq:hazard_integral} is the usual random-time-change or cumulative-hazard construction \citep{ethier1986,anderson2007}. Unconditionally, the macro-event process has a stochastic predictable intensity rather than the deterministic intensity of an ordinary non-homogeneous Poisson process. For constant $\Theta$,~\eqref{eq:hazard_integral} reduces to Gillespie's waiting time $(t^\ast-t_0)=-\ln U/\Theta$, $U\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$. Combined with exact local engines, this construction defines the \emph{ideal} two-scale process, whose transition rates are characterised in Section~\ref{sec:surrogate}.
+In words, the area under the rate curve grows until it first reaches the random threshold. If it never reaches the threshold, no further macro event occurs. This is the standard cumulative-rate construction \citep{ethier1986,anderson2007}. If $\Theta$ is constant, it gives the familiar Gillespie waiting time $(t^\ast-t_0)=-\ln U/\Theta$, where $U$ is uniform on $(0,1)$. Exact evaluation of this first crossing, together with exact local simulators, defines the ideal two-scale process. Section~\ref{sec:surrogate} shows that this ideal process is exact for the surrogate network.
 
-The implemented scheduler (Algorithm~\ref{alg:scheduler}) treats the local engines as black boxes that expose their state only when they are advanced. It divides time into synchronisation intervals of length $\tau$. Let $A$ denote the approximate cumulative macro hazard accumulated since the previous macro event. On each (sub)interval $[t,t_g)$, with $\Delta=t_g-t$, the scheduler estimates $\Theta$ by a midpoint probe: copies of all engines, including their pseudorandom-number states, are advanced to $t+\Delta/2$, and $\Theta_{\mathrm{mid}}$ is computed from those copies. If $A+\Theta_{\mathrm{mid}}\Delta<E$, the actual engines are advanced to $t_g$. Otherwise, the crossing time is interpolated as
+The implemented scheduler checks progress on intervals of length $\tau$. Let $A$ be the amount of macro rate already accumulated since the previous macro event. For the current interval $[t,t_g)$, it copies every local simulator, including its random-number state, and advances each copy to the midpoint. The copies give a midpoint estimate $\Theta_{\mathrm{mid}}$. If the estimated accumulation remains below $E$, the real simulators move to $t_g$. If it reaches $E$, the scheduler estimates the crossing time by
 \begin{equation}
 \label{eq:interpolation}
-t^\ast=t+\frac{E-A}{\Theta_{\mathrm{mid}}},
+t^\ast=t+\frac{E-A}{\Theta_{\mathrm{mid}}}.
 \end{equation}
-the engines are advanced to $t^\ast$, and the pair and target are sampled from their states at $t^\ast$. The threshold is then renewed and the remainder of the interval is processed in the same way.
+The numerator is the amount left before the threshold, and the denominator is the estimated rate. The scheduler then advances the real simulators to $t^\ast$, draws the source and receiving communities, selects the target node, and imports the infection. Algorithm~\ref{alg:scheduler} gives the full sequence.
 
