-Two properties of the scheduler matter for interpreting the results. First, because the midpoint probes copy the pseudorandom-number states, a probe reproduces the trajectory that the corresponding engine will actually follow up to the midpoint unless an import intervenes. The midpoint rule is therefore a quadrature of the realised piecewise-constant hazard. It is exact on subintervals containing no local event in any community; its quadrature error and its look-ahead of at most $\tau/2$ vanish as $\tau\to0$. Second, if local engines expose their next-event times, the black-box restriction is unnecessary. Since $\Theta$ is piecewise constant between local events, integral~\eqref{eq:hazard_integral} can then be evaluated exactly by an event-driven scheduler that merges the next-event times of all engines. This removes the numerical synchronisation approximation. We evaluate the grid-based variant, which is the implemented method, with $\tau=1$.
+The copied random-number states are important. Unless an import occurs first, a copied simulator follows the same path that the real simulator will follow to the midpoint. The midpoint estimate is exact on an interval with no local event. Its timing error and its look-ahead of at most $\tau/2$ decrease to zero as $\tau$ decreases.
+
+The time grid is needed because the scheduler treats local simulators as black boxes. If they also report their next local-event times, the scheduler can merge those times and accumulate the piecewise-constant value of $\Theta$ exactly. That event-driven version removes the numerical synchronisation approximation. The experiments in this article use the implemented grid version with $\tau=1$.
 
 \subsection{Computational structure and intended use}
 \label{sec:cost}
 
-\Full{} stores every channel, including all inter-community channels, and its cost per event grows with node degree \citep{kuryliak2025simulating}. \TS{} stores the within-community graphs and an $M\times M$ weight matrix. This is the saving emphasised in the workshop paper \citep{kuryliak2025towards}: when inter-community contacts are numerous, for example when a mobility flow corresponds to many individual contacts, the interface reduces to $O(M^2)$ numbers. Local engines can also be modified independently, which makes community-specific interventions straightforward to represent. Against this, the grid-synchronised scheduler adds a cost proportional to $(T_{\mathrm{end}}/\tau)\sum_iN_i$ for copying engine states, independent of the number of events. The method is therefore suited to settings in which the inter-community contacts are unknown or too numerous to represent, rather than to small networks whose full contact structure is available. Measured run times are reported in Section~\ref{sec:disc_cost}.
+\Full{} stores all local and cross-community channels, and its work per event grows with node degree \citep{kuryliak2025simulating}. \TS{} stores the local graphs and an $M\times M$ matrix of aggregate weights. This is the memory saving described in the workshop paper \citep{kuryliak2025towards}. It can matter when a mobility flow stands for many unknown individual contacts. Local simulators can also use different community-specific settings.
 
-%======================================================================
+The grid scheduler has its own cost because it copies local states at every time step. Therefore, the method is intended for cases where individual cross-community contacts are unavailable or too numerous to store. It is not expected to be faster for every known, small network. Section~\ref{sec:disc_cost} reports the measured run times; the main evaluation in this article concerns approximation error.
